#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/id #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7048" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/31069ee6b2fce087880c8d94ec10bbb724e5f1d07be9adc6040c0a6b3aa2ff8a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/8103dd1abfd1ba9488509a0e858113e05e877956d99e6b2d1eb1ac2bd6b85c7e.png)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/6425989fee48f2b78a4f2c8e1cbda9c0f64597600d2b0a054f9dea020d4e0f81.png)\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/c20e24c2457c66423fa2ff066a6d34dc1d5e2e044703236310f1a3f1aa48ce65.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7049" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/1ead7e2a9fff08d6ec1d8e70c3f9f0c10729a4899227c53eabe7a5212feb1659.png)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/36b5e6757a7849e9e405883da345dadfbcc3130b517d05e117f4fb6fb4338ae8.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/f9f940992f15a14e58f7eee0b8171e8e0e437787d6340618fcd1f23b84d333a8.png)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/9a7426e0e17bb57e1a8bfb24dca5ca3a579809708af3055184f9cfc2728957bc.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/f3e3bae74afa48fd689bb48dc317af52e44914f9f35d32375463d3705268b377.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/6e5c9442761d05d62ccc3ce56cbe09794e57b931987e3e4ef33d93d7cfb1e39f.png)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/6b54faff854b99c0c86b3de477cc2dde8b5a714bfc296c59ed6757f9bc250f77.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/96ee1e3a1af5c670b87cd06e406ac16b0fa9f5e5305b2fe95dc395c079d291da.png)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-7050" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/9459f772e062490c525f6c7bc083c02d0ed5dc3bc4c331f642653aa4f9bf5515.png)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/df4b98b4799865dbf40e765bda0fa6176157139d99a62bd1febe48d7c4600c8c.png)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/8032d75d584838d0743f2e9217c25844af637d07261dd2d6a89add9df531fdb1.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/aa5146c331315952e36051be1126010bbcaf706f2cbc1c886c56b0e92b468fa4.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/b0d075668031a0decad6d092f2f03c3f74eacdcff0546e99e8373901c5a910ac.png)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/726b5737809539337c1a8a259727a47b9bdacdf81de92a4a35783518df9dcaa0.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/a1a3e46d8352ceee549fb5040261d8edf036d8fb32fc5fc5b960cab07097970f.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/2e3a20e62437546fb2498d1575c9ed2cd268c43b968174ea7e0139977fb6fbd5.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e349736-e032-5c48-ac0e-33d84005d257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155226} {:answer/id #uuid "df12b381-f17f-5822-99ee-98fc05adaaf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155227} {:answer/id #uuid "f26dd776-3f42-58e2-990d-79c5942c99d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155228} {:answer/id #uuid "158d0b81-5987-5088-9a41-4b14ff7956a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155229} {:answer/id #uuid "49442852-dfad-553f-83ef-e8b645fd8491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df12b381-f17f-5822-99ee-98fc05adaaf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155227} :answer-field/id #uuid "cba6f085-8040-5c8d-a9c0-beeff1f1f4ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f38771d6-22f6-588d-95e5-d42dd40123e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325120" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab481a2e-415d-58d2-a997-4b68fd69003a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1 - t)e^{4t} \\\\ - (1 + 2t)e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155238} {:answer/id #uuid "d3724f08-8a3a-5f41-b9db-f993a966d4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1 - t)e^{4t} \\\\ -e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155239} {:answer/id #uuid "fd53afcd-195a-52a2-b1a2-4890ba8b57bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2 + t)e^{4t} \\\\ - (1 + 2t)e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155240} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1953aa-7928-552c-ae5c-0ac1b676a1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2e^{4t} \\\\ -e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155241} {:answer/id #uuid "701eb902-2e4a-562b-8d35-40c92adab2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2e^{4t} \\\\ - (1 + 2t)e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3724f08-8a3a-5f41-b9db-f993a966d4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1 - t)e^{4t} \\\\ -e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155239} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad6a1bb-d19b-5480-a9e2-344944bf4ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d755d77-3b2c-5d58-97ac-ef5c72049848" :question/math-academy-id "q-325135" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1b739f3-bfcb-5e13-8237-f4f67ff69eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-1.7t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355458} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a965e6-d8a5-5e11-8844-8149ae76fc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355459} {:answer/id #uuid "44f8511e-7f73-543b-b2df-2dc14db016be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355460} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3b9617-a16a-520f-883f-7c4d2d90bad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355461} {:answer/id #uuid "fffffc0e-599a-5271-b895-5afa7d6d626c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8a965e6-d8a5-5e11-8844-8149ae76fc60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355459} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd20c8b0-40cd-5de1-8e89-0b06c0608884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61c00866-3159-5d32-a067-dd30c3af3f25" :question/math-academy-id "q-326150" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.7\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd760e29-e0d7-5271-8e97-4820c5e20fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355465} {:answer/id #uuid "708fbda8-aecd-535b-9d1a-45206ce50ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355466} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0b8cde-2308-5684-bc2d-4170ce068ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355467} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9ea115-8728-5121-ba04-3e5a7925691f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355468} {:answer/id #uuid "41e889f0-cf67-56c8-bde3-9d09c0da1700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9ea115-8728-5121-ba04-3e5a7925691f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355468} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb54014a-0986-526c-b516-fae16665be31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17c5a462-c29c-5b82-b891-ee8c95c1220f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326236" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbfa898-030f-53c4-a770-0688e8877a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355472} {:answer/id #uuid "134b4c11-d326-522f-97ce-12148715b3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355473} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7e136f-f3b6-5428-9066-57e466c656d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355474} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a2e68c-e5d3-5cdc-ab13-cceb60956c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355475} {:answer/id #uuid "e330ff9b-0514-50dc-b0d3-ef44315232cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a2e68c-e5d3-5cdc-ab13-cceb60956c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355475} :answer-field/id #uuid "47271602-add0-5c44-b23d-e6f1a30f5d9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe888fa9-7251-5c8a-8852-7b62173b34d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-326238" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46d6c3ae-3d98-52a0-a106-822bbbdaa1f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355478} {:answer/id #uuid "23388662-e6bc-548f-9318-ba94193c1719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355479} {:answer/id #uuid "845f381d-537e-5c2d-bbb4-b8b7321a0a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355480} {:answer/id #uuid "8186f783-d856-5b27-9bf2-856239e0f51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355481} {:answer/id #uuid "1328166d-9d11-5b22-9725-6b84ac2aa3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8186f783-d856-5b27-9bf2-856239e0f51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355481} :answer-field/id #uuid "f676e95a-8101-5c9b-8f04-346e82ca354c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1a7a31a-a1ff-52db-b87c-67b3aaf6bbd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80548" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90fc2663-2388-54bb-b2d0-073ba822fa18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355485} {:answer/id #uuid "33e58783-2283-5311-8386-6027a8a24d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{t}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355486} {:answer/id #uuid "34a3044f-59ad-53bf-8673-cfe699b67018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355487} {:answer/id #uuid "73a18dc7-cd83-5c55-8c11-12b4224389f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355488} {:answer/id #uuid "df6c93d3-71c2-5f32-b270-eb09df4b521f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df6c93d3-71c2-5f32-b270-eb09df4b521f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355489} :answer-field/id #uuid "971c2a2c-0681-507f-ac6b-d70e10bd4be9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a3c852b-7784-5a95-a0d4-cd03c258f439" :question/math-academy-id "q-80601" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0103f426-58bd-53b1-8f97-80038abf715f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355492} {:answer/id #uuid "00531d2c-3516-58b1-9ee5-e740fba747cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355493} {:answer/id #uuid "301a2df2-9c2b-5931-a8b8-4c9ed8cfa30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355494} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc722e9-d6cf-508e-8b52-eab0ecfe90c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355495} {:answer/id #uuid "28705cca-ef75-51ce-869d-bb8857e86d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0103f426-58bd-53b1-8f97-80038abf715f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355492} :answer-field/id #uuid "5362545c-1654-545d-afac-90b1c01533e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a890d7a7-d6a7-598c-b8de-0c6c86cd59af" :question/math-academy-id "q-80603" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-2.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1+2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=1,$ and $a+b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492847c3-dcfc-554c-bf56-ed40f02b273a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355499} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddbff16-0ab8-548a-b391-0173fa194350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355500} {:answer/id #uuid "aa865f13-e07d-58b4-aa92-8898251eed51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355501} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e4ffa9-4805-59d5-887b-04dbcca8e8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355502} {:answer/id #uuid "17f315b1-aeb4-5689-9bd5-8085507f8038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa865f13-e07d-58b4-aa92-8898251eed51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355501} :answer-field/id #uuid "164061c8-7e18-5b2b-b508-6a3649ea0889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e333c35f-41be-5fac-830b-452f74b604a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80604" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1+1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=-2,b=-1,$ and $a+b=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb1237f4-f879-5dd1-900c-fa616c3085b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355506} {:answer/id #uuid "442c9958-715b-5413-900b-50347d126a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355507} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9be16e-8125-5fa4-af4f-5055978019e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355508} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee19b2e-7bd1-5f99-adee-2e97d56781b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355509} {:answer/id #uuid "ed19242a-7294-578e-b6e9-e0bf9bedda90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9be16e-8125-5fa4-af4f-5055978019e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355508} :answer-field/id #uuid "578925d7-bb42-5dc7-abc8-18ced5053b8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c35a2dcf-409f-52ac-b982-e1b503b77b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-80910" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46793ab-e177-5ba2-b494-1566ac4bea6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355513} {:answer/id #uuid "4903b97b-4de9-5db0-8679-82ef4aeb80b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355514} {:answer/id #uuid "af357a51-26c3-534a-b1c5-1b063e651f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355515} {:answer/id #uuid "81fd647c-fdb5-5e18-ad3d-e86d0e1b5976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-10t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355516} {:answer/id #uuid "d2126331-87c8-5edd-b3a1-7e05f7b3f6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2126331-87c8-5edd-b3a1-7e05f7b3f6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355517} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a89ca1-14d5-52b9-bf97-08e617a49824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da5fe32-3bcb-5f06-97cf-07344e577a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80913" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1e93ef0-99c8-57b5-ab92-0e800f72f829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355520} {:answer/id #uuid "abe01502-a8e9-532e-99d1-ac6d462ec225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355521} {:answer/id #uuid "ab001922-1dd5-5363-9b1c-28e454d163c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355522} {:answer/id #uuid "2459e0e0-6f11-590f-9fbb-bcb31ad40784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355523} {:answer/id #uuid "39b27585-57bc-5073-b959-116b10048a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2459e0e0-6f11-590f-9fbb-bcb31ad40784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355523} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a73b09b-57ca-506d-bd89-d5407de2d78d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81977095-c899-5440-9ace-acb02a20d076" :question/math-academy-id "q-80915" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3+3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3+3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d02c77c-55c9-55aa-8dd2-06ac710b4330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1f34d1-61b8-5852-bb1f-fe4a52271ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355528} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b590e2-00e2-58e0-918a-0094a50af2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355529} {:answer/id #uuid "73dbd3b8-e582-504f-94c3-613783472205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355530} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd3cde3-91ee-5ee2-89e3-54f96363400d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfd3cde3-91ee-5ee2-89e3-54f96363400d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355531} :answer-field/id #uuid "a946fe20-eb34-5f9d-a921-6e03a2e9b62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5838657b-dc89-531e-ae47-d32a758d2632" :question/math-academy-id "q-80927" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59506bab-0a27-59c4-8f73-591c096a3f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355534} {:answer/id #uuid "37a0af68-6090-5ecb-a178-860e43900747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355535} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1fd842-1648-5066-856a-9582a7ab36b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355536} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e3d8c5-e01f-5fdf-97b9-70fad47119e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355537} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb8af7d-013e-5804-a11b-663c2abe992b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1fd842-1648-5066-856a-9582a7ab36b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355536} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b09de5-2286-53e7-9771-e6bd71e7f5ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e25df6c-8786-547c-9562-9085a352e6d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80928" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+5([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57c196c2-cb78-57bf-a36b-425feafc3f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355541} {:answer/id #uuid "71ad5a35-a67d-5696-a367-cd2054d6b9cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355542} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3faae3-6f6a-50ce-8639-f65fcb12fd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355543} {:answer/id #uuid "b129411a-ce21-5b50-911b-e734a9855459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355544} {:answer/id #uuid "e65acff9-5c57-57f1-8f63-35b401255e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57c196c2-cb78-57bf-a36b-425feafc3f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355541} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb09622f-d721-5933-9b4d-388a9d7b17b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "238c50de-f678-5486-9e97-0de67f7c80f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80932" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =7.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}-3[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
